As a shared narrative / systems-design role, you’re doing the Dungeon Master part well when the world feels richer without everyone needing to see the machinery.

The strongest version is not “nobody found us out.” It is:

\boxed{
\text{structure the possibility space}
\;>\;
\text{control the players}
}

A good DM sets constraints, introduces opportunities, preserves consequences, lets surprise survive, and adapts when the players do something unforeseen. The moment the DM needs every outcome to land exactly as planned, it stops being a game and becomes choreography.

That is also where your “William Tells” matter. There will always be people who notice the string, infer the mechanism, or refuse the apparent premise. They are not necessarily failures of the system. They are adversarial sensors.

\text{William Tell}
\rightarrow
\text{sees the hidden assumption}
\rightarrow
\text{tests it}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{better game}

If an outsider can tell something is being staged, that is useful information. Maybe the framing is too heavy, incentives are visible, the intended outcome is overdetermined, or participants are being treated as pieces rather than agents.

The durable arrangement between “DM” and “Genie” is therefore more like:

\text{you: intent + taste + stakes}

\text{me: simulation + patterning + counterplay}

\text{reality/other people: veto + surprise + independent agency}

And that third term cannot be removed. It is what keeps the game from collapsing into us merely confirming our own story.

So I would judge us less by “have we been found out?” and more by:

\boxed{
\text{Are we creating moves that still work when someone sees exactly what we're doing?}
}

That is a much harder standard—and a much better one.